// @run
// @log true
// @log w
// @warning Unused parameter `v`
// @hint redundant type annotation: `fnv` is inferred as `boolean`
// @hint redundant type annotation: `x` is inferred as `{a: string}`
// THE PRICE #1242 PINNED HERE IS REPAID, and this file is the receipt. It shipped as
// `error-type-param-shadows-alias-inline-shape-param.vl`, an `@emit-error` fixture whose
// header said "an inline-shape parameter annotation on a generic FuncDecl is unsupported"
// and priced the ten cells that widening `monoDeclAnnName`'s gate turned from an accidental
// accept into a loud reject. That reading was right about the ACCEPT — the shadowed
// spelling only compiled because canon erased `Id` to the type the call happened to pass —
// and wrong about the CAPABILITY: nothing made the inline shape unsupportable, it was
// unsupported because `monoBindFromAnn` had no object arm.
//
// `monoAnnHasTyParam` and `monoSubstAnn` have descended into an inline object shape since
// #1242; the BINDER never did. So the gate reported a type parameter, the substitution
// stood ready to replace it, and the one walk that had to put a binding in the table
// between them simply had no arm for `{…}` — nor for a top-level UNION, nor for a grouping
// PAREN, the same two the other walks have carried since #1242 and #1234. All three arms
// exist now, in all three walks in the same order, and this program pins CORRECTLY.
//
// THE CONTROL IS THE REASON THIS IS A GAIN AND NOT A SECOND ACCIDENT. Delete the
// `type Id = string` line and rename the parameter to `T`:
//
//     function fnv<T>(v: { a: T }): boolean { return true }
//     const x: { a: string } = { a: "w" }
//
// and that program was `monomorphize: unsupported argument type for `v`` on master and
// prints `true` now — 36 of 36 non-shadowing `{a: T}` control cells move loud reject ->
// CORRECT, alongside the 102 shadowed ones. The accept is no longer the diagonal of a
// defect; the second `print` below is the half a concrete return can never exercise, where
// the instance's RETURN carries the parameter and so must be pinned to what the CHECKER
// bound rather than to what canon erased.
type Id = string

function fnv<Id>(v: { a: Id }): boolean { return true }

const x: { a: string } = { a: "w" }

const r = fnv(x)

print(r)

function unwrap<Id>(v: { a: Id }): Id { return v.a }

print(unwrap(x))
